#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142610 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07e8cbe2-1fe8-5038-9b0a-061070ffe1fb" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions With Equal Denominators"}] :question/id #uuid "ae470aa4-327d-5b21-9e11-217710f1ef94" :question/math-academy-id "e-13787" :question/problem "![](images/91/916d8a6c0c4ba6c249191c0ee0d85d20f3e14ebfbabbdb0a6a5be583e39be139.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8}$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/ea/ea208841c3276de2e8736add1ad5c6f4c343a9ec8d137f74f10e47b9243e87eb.png)\n\nWe can break down our division problem as follows:\n\n- Computing $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ means we want to determine how many times $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$ fits into $\\dfrac{\\color{red}5}{8}$.\n- Since both fractions have a *common denominator*, this is the *same* as determining how many times ${\\color{blue}3}$ fits into ${\\color{red}5}$.\n- Therefore, instead of $\\dfrac{{\\color{red}5}}{8}\\div\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{8}$, we can simply compute ${\\color{red}5}\\div {\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}5} \\div {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{8} \\div \\dfrac{3}{8} = \\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142611 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142824 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fae4d19-7336-5557-8df9-bc8e0b6703fa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Missing Number"}] :question/id #uuid "0b51ee70-1d44-5427-8b9f-3930dc3a1962" :question/math-academy-id "e-13790" :question/problem "![](images/c5/c5e7367d6b3a53b513c2921d91579b1bd6588b850622ecf1c7b370695227a64d.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the equation below.\n$6$" :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $5$ and $10$. We can make a common denominator of $10$ by splitting the shape on the left into $2$ parts vertically:\n\n![](images/3d/3d4839662bcac31bc197436b533da61e07ecf1ca301b3fa7613d433833e14028.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}6}{10} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}7}{10}\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}6} \\div {\\color{blue}7} = \\dfrac{6}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n6\n$$\n\nHence, the missing number is $6$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142612 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142825 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ab62b651-0d87-5ed3-b749-11d1e25be6a8" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions Using Models"}] :question/id #uuid "daa61f81-4882-5af9-b86a-8b03a586e93b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13792" :question/problem "![](images/87/87ce51fed33e907d6acb7e213bec596ea97b16e739bb96b5446975dc6a593011.png)\n\nCalculate $\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4}$ using the model above." :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $4$ and $3$. We can make a common denominator of\n\n$$\n4\\times 3 = 12\n$$\n\nWe split the shape on the left of the division sign into $4$ parts vertically, and we split the shape on the right into $3$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/93/931f2220f4f5b4e8d3760bcf283d19ef565d2bd8910db6d95cd44e604782a0f0.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}8}{12} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}9}{12}\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}8} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = \\dfrac{8}{9}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\div \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{8}{9} \\,\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142826 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50b35123-63ff-559f-b9b6-61ea6915ce0a" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Fractions When the Result Is a Mixed Number"}] :question/id #uuid "066a9329-d63e-55d4-bc6b-bcdb5dd96cc3" :question/math-academy-id "e-13793" :question/problem "![](images/93/93a73e4b7458251fdb96891e2b00af4fd5ecf6a442aed9fb7c289ca25ae0e389.png)\n\nUse the model above to find the value of $\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7}$." :question/worked-solution "The denominators are $7$ and $14$. We can make a common denominator of $14$.\n\nWe split the shape on the right of the division sign into $2$ parts horizontally:\n\n![](images/a9/a9867f3e62605ccbaad6fdc6a1925173a460f287e36b8cc563ab62bb3074a13c.png)\n\nSo, our division problem now is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{\\color{blue}6}{14}\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are equal, this is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}11} \\div {\\color{blue}6} = 1\\,\\dfrac56\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{11}{14} \\div \\dfrac{3}{7} = 1\\,\\dfrac56 \\,\n$$"}]}
